            <ArticleTitle>[Expression of mitogen-activated protein kinase in vascular tissues after coronary artery balloon injury in rat].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>469-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To observe the changes in mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) activity and gene expression after coronary artery balloon injury in rat.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Forty Wistar rats were randomly divided into control group (without coronary artery balloon injury), and 3, 7 and 14 days after coronary artery balloon injury groups (n=10, respectively). The activity of MAPK was measured by biochemical method and the gene expression was examined by reverse transcription-polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">MAPK activity and gene expression of MAPK in the coronary artery balloon injury in rat after 3, 7 and 14 days were significantly higher than that of normal control, especially 3 days after injury [MAPK activity: (17.32+/-2.17) pmol x mg(-1) x min(-1);MAPK protein: ERK1 was 194.7+/-8.6, ERK2 was 175.8+/-7.9; MAPK gene expression: ERK1 was 1.15+/-0.21, ERK2 was 1.13+/-0.14 ].</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The increase activity and mRNA of MAPK may be involved in the restenosis process after coronary balloon injury.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
